encountered to date.

39:05.000 --> 39:10.000
So let me explain how I
discovered that my podcast feed
was hijacked,

39:10.000 --> 39:14.000
and why I think RSS hijacking is
so insidious.

39:14.000 --> 39:23.000
A couple of months ago, I read
on slash dot that Yahoo had
released a new podcast search
engine at podcast.yahoo.com.

39:23.000 --> 39:28.000
I went to that site and was
pleased to see that Eric Steiner
already had a listing.

39:28.000 --> 39:36.000
But on this listing, I noticed
that the RSS feed listed for my
show was not hosted by my
website vegan.com,

39:36.000 --> 39:43.000
but instead was hosted by some
website I'd never even heard of
called podkeyword.com.

39:43.000 --> 39:46.000
Now, what happened was obvious.

39:46.000 --> 39:53.000
And random guy up there owning
the podkeyword.com domain had
created an RSS feed on his site

39:53.000 --> 39:56.000
and pointed it to my RSS feed.

39:56.000 --> 40:01.000
And somehow Yahoo had picked up
his RSS feed instead of my own

40:01.000 --> 40:06.000
and inserted it into its
directory listing for my show.

40:06.000 --> 40:10.000
Now, I'm not the most
technically savvy person you'll
ever meet,

40:10.000 --> 40:13.000
but I could immediately
recognize the danger here.

40:13.000 --> 40:20.000
Over time, more and more Yahoo
users would subscribe to my show
using an RSS feed that belonged
not to me,

40:20.000 --> 40:22.000
but to a third party.

40:22.000 --> 40:27.000
Now, I'm from New York and
perhaps overly suspicious about
people's motives,

40:27.000 --> 40:32.000
